1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:03,821
We start with a simple request about the
order validator.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,013
A chat response can explain the code you
provide.

3
00:00:07,193 --> 00:00:11,685
An agent session can also search the
workspace, propose edits, and run

4
00:00:11,685 --> 00:00:14,765
permitted checks, depending on the tools
it has.

5
00:00:14,945 --> 00:00:19,511
Open an approved training workspace and
look at the actions shown in the session.

6
00:00:19,691 --> 00:00:23,925
The proposed answer and the actions taken
are both worth reviewing.

7
00:00:25,382 --> 00:00:27,218
An agent works through a loop.

8
00:00:27,398 --> 00:00:32,769
It collects relevant information, proposes
or takes an allowed action, looks at the

9
00:00:32,769 --> 00:00:36,198
result, and decides whether another action
is needed.

10
00:00:36,378 --> 00:00:41,654
In our case, it reads the issue, inspects
the validator, and finds the missing test.

11
00:00:41,834 --> 00:00:46,974
We can follow each step because the task
has a narrow scope and a clear place to

12
00:00:46,974 --> 00:00:47,295
stop.

13
00:00:48,757 --> 00:00:54,086
Ask the agent to investigate why zero is
accepted and to suggest the smallest

14
00:00:54,086 --> 00:00:54,570
change.

15
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:57,441
Keep the task at planning in the approved
session.

16
00:00:57,621 --> 00:01:02,111
We expect the agent to point to one
comparison and describe one test.

17
00:01:02,291 --> 00:01:07,090
If it suggests redesigning validation,
steer it back to the boundary case.

18
00:01:07,270 --> 00:01:10,801
Check what tools the session can use, even
during planning.

19
00:01:12,257 --> 00:01:16,844
Now the training agent proposes running
the unit tests in the authorized

20
00:01:16,844 --> 00:01:17,481
workspace.

21
00:01:17,661 --> 00:01:22,249
Read the command, its working directory,
and the reason before deciding.

22
00:01:22,429 --> 00:01:26,983
The command should match the task and
operate on the sample repository.

23
00:01:27,163 --> 00:01:32,372
Pause when an action would touch unrelated
paths or make an unrequested external

24
00:01:32,372 --> 00:01:32,827
change.

25
00:01:33,007 --> 00:01:37,241
The approval controls available to you
depend on company settings.

26
00:01:38,715 --> 00:01:42,928
The test run is useful when it reports the
assertion we meant to check.

27
00:01:43,108 --> 00:01:48,049
In this synthetic example, the original
condition accepts zero and the new

28
00:01:48,049 --> 00:01:49,518
regression test fails.

29
00:01:49,698 --> 00:01:54,349
We read the reported test name and failure
to confirm it matches the issue.

30
00:01:54,529 --> 00:01:59,224
If a setup problem caused the failure
instead, diagnose that problem before

31
00:01:59,224 --> 00:02:00,726
interpreting the result.

32
00:02:02,173 --> 00:02:05,703
Keep track of the environment when you
move between sessions.

33
00:02:05,883 --> 00:02:11,961
In this course, Copilot is in VS Code, and
Claude Code or OpenCode can be available

34
00:02:11,961 --> 00:02:14,670
inside the approved remote workspace.

35
00:02:14,850 --> 00:02:18,594
A visible tool approval belongs to the
session that offered it.

36
00:02:18,774 --> 00:02:24,443
Verify the repository and environment
before letting an agent touch files or run

37
00:02:24,443 --> 00:02:24,939
checks.

38
00:02:26,382 --> 00:02:28,921
Give the training agent a bounded task.

39
00:02:29,101 --> 00:02:34,464
Say what files it may inspect, what action
it can take, what evidence to return, and

40
00:02:34,464 --> 00:02:35,294
when to stop.

41
00:02:35,474 --> 00:02:39,272
Then review the actual output and explain
what it established.

42
00:02:39,452 --> 00:02:44,667
For this exercise, success is a focused
plan and the expected test evidence.

43
00:02:44,847 --> 00:02:48,612
Any further publication can be treated as
a separate decision.
